             b"\3i\1)\6a=\13\0\0Directory tree generator.\12\12    For each directory i"
             b"n the directory tree rooted at top (including top\12    itself, but excl"
             b"uding '.' and '..'), yields a 3-tuple\12\12        dirpath, dirnames, fi"
             b"lenames\12\12    dirpath is a string, the path to the directory.  dirnam"
             b"es is a list of\12    the names of the subdirectories in dirpath (includ"
             b"ing symlinks to\12    directories, and excluding '.' and '..').\12    fi"
             b"lenames is a list of the names of the non-directory files in dirpath.\12"
             b"    Note that the names in the lists are just names, with no path\12    "
             b"components.  To get a full path (which begins with top) to a file or\12 "
             b"   directory in dirpath, do os.path.join(dirpath, name).\12\12    If opt"
             b"ional arg 'topdown' is true or not specified, the triple for a\12    dir"
             b"ectory is generated before the triples for any of its subdirectories\12 "
             b"   (directories are generated top down).  If topdown is false, the tripl"
             b"e\12    for a directory is generated after the triples for all of its\12"
             b"    subdirectories (directories are generated bottom up).\12\12    When "
             b"topdown is true, the caller can modify the dirnames list in-place\12    "
             b"(e.g., via del or slice assignment), and walk will only recurse into the"
             b"\12    subdirectories whose names remain in dirnames; this can be used t"
             b"o prune\12    the search, or to impose a specific order of visiting.  Mo"
             b"difying\12    dirnames when topdown is false has no effect on the behavi"
             b"or of\12    os.walk(), since the directories in dirnames have already be"
             b"en generated\12    by the time dirnames itself is generated. No matter t"
             b"he value of\12    topdown, the list of subdirectories is retrieved befor"
             b"e the tuples for\12    the directory and its subdirectories are generate"
             b"d.\12\12    By default errors from the os.scandir() call are ignored.  I"
             b"f\12    optional arg 'onerror' is specified, it should be a function; it"
             b"\12    will be called with one argument, an OSError instance.  It can\12"
             b"    report the error to continue with the walk, or raise the exception"
             b"\12    to abort the walk.  Note that the filename is available as the\12"
             b"    filename attribute of the exception object.\12\12    By default, os."
             b"walk does not follow symbolic links to subdirectories on\12    systems t"
             b"hat support them.  In order to get this functionality, set the\12    opt"
             b"ional argument 'followlinks' to true.\12\12    Caution:  if you pass a r"
             b"elative pathname for top, don't change the\12    current working directo"
             b"ry between resumptions of walk.  walk never\12    changes the current di"
             b"rectory, and assumes that the client doesn't\12    either.\12\12    Exam"
             b"ple:\12\12    import os\12    from os.path import join, getsize\12    fo"
             b"r root, dirs, files in os.walk('python/Lib/xml'):\12        print(root, "
             b"\"consumes \")\12        print(sum(getsize(join(root, name)) for name in"
             b" files), end=\" \")\12        print(\"bytes in\", len(files), \"non-dire"
             b"ctory files\")\12        if '__pycache__' in dirs:\12            dirs.re"
             b"move('__pycache__')  # don't visit __pycache__ directories\12\12    z\7o"
             b"s.walkF\251\1\332\17follow_symlinksTN)\22\332\3sys\332\5audit\332\6fspat"
             b"hre\0\0\0\332\6islink\332\4join\332\3poprj\0\0\0\332\5tuplerP\0\0\0\332"
